{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_1>\nimport sys\nimport re\n\nline = sys.stdin.readline().rstrip('\\n')\nfirst_char = line[0]\nrest = line[1:]\n\n# Find the first occurrence of \"at\" in the rest\nmatch = re.search(r'at', rest)\nif match:\n    at_pos = match.start()\n    before_at = rest[:at_pos+2]\n    after_at = rest[at_pos+2:]\nelse:\n    before_at = rest\n    after_at = \"\"\n\n# Replace \"dot\" with \".\" in both parts\nbefore_at = before_at.replace(\"dot\", \".\")\nafter_at = after_at.replace(\"dot\", \".\")\n\n# Replace \"at\" with \"@\" in the before_at part\nbefore_at = before_at.replace(\"at\", \"@\")\n\nprint first_char + before_at + after_at\n</your_rewritten_code_1>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport sys\nimport re\n\nline = sys.stdin.readline().rstrip('\\n')\n\n# We need to find exactly one '@' and any number of '.' in the email\n# The input contains \"at\" and \"dot\" that need to be replaced\n\n# Strategy: try all possible positions for the '@' symbol (each \"at\" in the string)\n# For each candidate '@' position, replace all other \"dot\" with '.' and ensure the result is valid\n\ndef is_valid_email(s):\n    if not s:\n        return False\n    if s[0] == '.' or s[-1] == '.':\n        return False\n    if s[0] == '@' or s[-1] == '@':\n        return False\n    if s.count('@') != 1:\n        return False\n    # Check that all characters are valid: lowercase letters, '.', '@'\n    for c in s:\n        if not (c == '.' or c == '@' or ('a' <= c <= 'z')):\n            return False\n    return True\n\n# Generate all possible email addresses by choosing one \"at\" to become '@'\n# and replacing all \"dot\" with '.' (except those that are part of \"at\")\n\ncandidates = []\n\n# Find all occurrences of \"at\" in the string\nat_positions = []\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(line) - 1:\n    if line[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_positions.append(i)\n        i += 2\n    else:\n        i += 1\n\n# For each \"at\" position, create a candidate email\nfor at_pos in at_positions:\n    # Replace all \"dot\" with '.' first, but be careful about overlapping\n    # We'll build the string by processing character by character\n    result = []\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(line):\n        if i == at_pos:\n            # This is our chosen '@' position\n            result.append('@')\n            i += 2  # Skip 'at'\n        elif i < len(line) - 2 and line[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            # Replace 'dot' with '.'\n            result.append('.')\n            i += 3\n        else:\n            result.append(line[i])\n            i += 1\n    \n    candidate = ''.join(result)\n    if is_valid_email(candidate):\n        candidates.append(candidate)\n\n# Also consider the case where there might be no \"at\" in the original string\n# But the problem states there should be exactly one '@', so we must have at least one \"at\"\n# If no valid candidate found, we need to handle edge cases\n\n# If no candidates found, try to handle special cases\nif not candidates:\n    # Try replacing all \"dot\" with '.' and see if we can find a valid pattern\n    # This might happen if the input doesn't contain \"at\" but should still be processed\n    # But per problem, email must contain exactly one '@', so we need to find \"at\"\n    # If no \"at\" exists, we cannot form a valid email\n    pass\n\n# Find the shortest candidate, and if tie, lexicographically smallest\nif candidates:\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (len(x), x))\n    print candidates[0]\nelse:\n    # Fallback: try to process the string in a different way\n    # Replace all \"dot\" with '.' and all but one \"at\" with something else?\n    # Actually, the problem says we have to carry out replacements to get a valid email\n    # If no valid email can be formed, we might need to handle differently\n    # But per problem constraints, input should allow a valid email\n    \n    # Let's try a simpler approach: replace all \"dot\" with '.' and replace first \"at\" with '@'\n    result = []\n    i = 0\n    at_replaced = False\n    while i < len(line):\n        if i < len(line) - 2 and line[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            result.append('.')\n            i += 3\n        elif i < len(line) - 1 and line[i:i+2] == 'at' and not at_replaced:\n            result.append('@')\n            at_replaced = True\n            i += 2\n        else:\n            result.append(line[i])\n            i += 1\n    \n    print ''.join(result)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.7\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -0.00098204065579921, -9.536738616588991e-07, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.038428422063589096, -0.0002416080387774855, -0.34845584630966187, -0.0007489498239010572, -0.00017629499780014157, -1.3351351299206726e-05, -0.0970378965139389, 0.0, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.2636104656849056e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.0010340826120227575, -3.933898824470816e-06, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -0.00980076752603054, -0.0018822111887857318, -0.30644047260284424, -0.004357961006462574, -7.867782187531702e-06, -0.01726057380437851, -0.40438026189804077, -3.516612196108326e-05, -0.5124397873878479, -7.390948667307384e-06, -1.1010017395019531, -1.2122052907943726, -1.9073304429184645e-05, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.04833054542541504, -0.02970227785408497, -0.020331313833594322, -0.6425900459289551, -0.0030942915473133326, -6.437280717364047e-06, -0.018169043585658073, -0.3385030925273895, -0.6186346411705017, -0.17913851141929626, -0.0006299418746493757, -1.9907753085135482e-05, -0.4153710603713989, -7.152555099310121e-07, -2.145764938177308e-06, -5.006777428206988e-06, -0.3304479122161865, -0.0033865978475660086, -6.6756979322235566e-06, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -0.0023319926112890244, -0.2052614390850067, -0.00028534632292576134, -0.1390267014503479, -0.12692910432815552, -0.7099339365959167, -0.07074745744466782, -0.0034577602054923773, -0.10398310422897339, -0.004225493408739567, -0.0758480653166771, 